The Python binding loads the installed shared pancake archives through `ctypes`
and mirrors the à-la-carte archive set. The core
[`bindings/python/pancake`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/python/pancake)
package (over `libpancake_ocapn`) presents a `Peer`. One submodule per
transport — `pancake.tcp`, `pancake.inproc`, and the lazy `pancake.iroh` —
loads only its own archive. The raw `ctypes` layer is the package's `_`-private
per-archive `_ffi_core` / `_ffi_tcp` / `_ffi_inproc` / `_ffi_iroh` modules. Wire
values map to native Python values (integers, strings, bytes, lists, and the
rest), so you rarely think about the FFI underneath.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip — connect, fetch, call,
handle the reply — reads like this in Python. `serve` keeps the connection
dispatching until close; `fetch` and `send` start the inbound loop on demand.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```python
> from pancake import connect, tcp_locator
>
> # tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> partner = tcp_locator("127.0.0.1", 9099)
>
> conn = await connect(partner)
> conn.serve()
> greeter = await conn.fetch("greeter")  # a capability, by name
> try:
>     reply = await conn.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"])
>     print(reply)
> except Exception as reason:
>     print(f"the greeter refused: {reason}")
> await conn.close()
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```python
> from pancake import connect
> from pancake.iroh import Reachability
>
> # iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. DISCOVERY resolves
> # the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh"
>
> conn = await connect(partner, Reachability.DISCOVERY)
> conn.serve()
> greeter = await conn.fetch("greeter")  # a capability, by name
> try:
>     reply = await conn.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"])
>     print(reply)
> except Exception as reason:
>     print(f"the greeter refused: {reason}")
> await conn.close()
> ```

The iroh tab swaps the built host-and-port locator for a bare node id and adds
the posture import; everything below `connect` is the tcp tab, line for line. The node id itself still arrives out of band, exactly like a
host and port; discovery turns a name you were given into a route, it does
not browse for peers.

## Async

The async surface is [`asyncio`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html).
The quick-start's `Connection` is the surface you hold: `await conn.send(ref,
verb, args)` awaits a result there. Beneath it, an `AsyncConnection` drives the
peer over a transport pump (asyncio streams for TCP) inside an `async with`
block, and exposes the raw events as `async for event in conn`. One connection
owns one peer and lives on one task, so the peer calls never block the event
loop; only the socket awaits.

## Actors

The actor layer is a `Vat` over `behavior(verb, args)` callables. A local actor is
an ordinary Python callable, and a leading symbol argument selects the method verb.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build python-dogfood    # the Python consumer against the pancake archives
```

The full surface lives in the
[Python binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/python/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/python/room_client.py)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
